    "case_id": "amb1-case01-product-scope",
    "focal_region": {
      "annotated_sentence_text": "[Turn 6 USER]\ne050\tNorthline emailed this morning.\ne051\tThey can live with their manual CSV process for another quarter, although their operations lead may move to a different role by then.\ne052\tThat reduces the urgency, but it also means we could lose the person who understands the problem best.\ne053\tOur smaller customers are not asking for reporting.\ne054\tThey are asking for fewer confusing states and faster support.\ne055\tI am less sure this is a broad product signal than I was yesterday, but more sure the discovery could be valuable.\n[Turn 6 ASSISTANT]\ne056\tThe new email separates two clocks: delivery can wait, but access to a knowledgeable collaborator may not.\ne057\tThat supports a narrower move—capture the workflow and test the reusable concept now without beginning full implementation.\ne058\tIt also strengthens the case for protecting the smaller-customer and reliability commitments, since their demand is current and repeated.\ne059\tThe open question is whether your team can conduct discovery without it becoming an unofficial project through momentum alone.\n[Turn 7 USER]\ne060\tMy provisional plan is to offer Northline three focused sessions over the next two weeks, document the workflow, and explicitly make no build commitment.\ne061\tLina can lead the sessions but not start production code.\ne062\tIn the same period we will define the reliability work and the core-workflow release we are actually willing to promise.\ne063\tThen we decide with all three pieces visible.\ne064\tI still worry that “no production code” wastes Lina's best way of learning and that Northline will hear a polite no.\ne065\tBut starting the build now feels less defensible than it did.\n[Turn 7 ASSISTANT]\ne066\tThat plan protects the learning opportunity without pretending the underlying tradeoff is resolved.\ne067\tThe two risks you named remain real: restricting prototypes may make discovery artificial, while careful language may still feel like distance to Northline.\ne068\tYou can decide in advance what kind of disposable prototype is allowed and ask Northline to reflect back what they believe the sessions mean.\ne069\tNeither step guarantees trust or a good product decision, but both make misunderstanding easier to notice before momentum chooses for you.",
